<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Malawi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lake Nyasa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a>, also called Lake Nyasa, is a long lake in the southern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-african-rift">East African Rift</a>, bordered by Malawi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> and Tanzania. Its shores were home to the Maravi states and later to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngoni">Ngoni</a>, Yao and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> rulers who traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slaves to the coast. Missions and British rule from the 1880s led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> of Nyasaland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-malawi.jpg" alt="Lake Malawi" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_Malawi_seen_from_orbit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
